#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176996 :tutorial/content "Given a matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $k$ is a vector $\\mathbf{v}$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I)^k \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}\n\\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad\n(A - \\lambda I)^{k-1} \\mathbf{v} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn how to calculate generalized eigenvectors of a specific rank for a given matrix $A.$ \n\nAs an example, consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/10/10d38f0f679ee090d30c89b7e2d161a788d406aabc17739e0bd80cd50b44b2dc.svg)\n\nSuppose we're given that $\\lambda = -3$ is an eigenvalue of $A,$ and that this eigenvalue has corresponding generalized eigenvectors of rank $2.$ How do we find one of these generalized eigenvectors?\n\nTo find a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda = -3,$ we need to find a vector $\\mathbf v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A + 3I)^2 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}\n\\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad\n(A +3I)^{} \\mathbf{v} \\neq \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each condition:\n\n- We start by solving $(A + 3I)^2 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}.$ First, we have\n![](images/7d/7d73d728223601f88133db9ba16477b2210b205e741d8948c7918da63f7d08ae.svg)\nComputing its square, we see that this matrix is nilpotent:\n![](images/b9/b9403343cc87da5373ac8dae651388c90c3f90a27f40b23216bb7c60c612dfd7.svg)\nThis means that *any* vector in $\\mathbb R^2$ is a solution to $(A + 3I)^2 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}.$ Therefore, the solution to $(A + 3I)^2 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=\\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}={v}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{v}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $(A +3I) \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}.$ We have\n![](images/bc/bcfadf0132e10c0f90f13a1938c3ae6d679b625e197bd4f977538a7923b2cd1a.svg)\nConverting this matrix to reduced row echelon form, we get\n![](images/85/85f41c64f2433d00fcac86d336f4b95d58a315ecf6dc9724953152301c58c4b0.svg)\nThis means the solution to $(A +3I) \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ is $v_2 = 0$, where $v_1$ is a free variable.  Therefore, the solution to $(A + 3I) \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=\\begin{bmatrix}{v}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}={v}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing our two solutions, we see that the vector\n\n![](images/cf/cff85d0846c6f53028f4cfae1b3d17feb79c0cac36a92addeb88393f6430df97.svg)\n\nsatisfies $(A +3I)^2 \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}$ but does *not* satisfy $(A +3 I) \\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{0}.$ \n\nHence, ![](images/5b/5b9968cb8a6950c87d94bdf617cb6d297ddfeac315805f68008dfd7fff0fa923.svg) is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ of $A.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e3b0674e-a304-5e37-b45a-b512efd3ba3a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5423 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
